---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
import { linkProps } from '../lib/site-ui';

const certs = [
  { name: 'AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Professional', year: 'Nov 2026' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified DevOps Engineer – Professional', year: 'May 2026' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Advanced Networking – Specialty', year: 'Aug 2025' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified SysOps Administrator – Associate', year: 'Feb 2025' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate', year: 'Mar 2024' },
  { name: 'AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner', year: 'Nov 2021' },
];

// Front-loaded facts for a quick skim.
const glance = [
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Cloud Software Engineer, John Deere · since 2019' },
  { k: 'Based', v: 'Des Moines, Iowa' },
  { k: 'Focus', v: 'Helping Frontier Teams move faster and more reliably' },
  {
    k: 'Currently',
    v: 'Running coding agents in the cloud, building a shared knowledge base for humans and agents, and surfacing AI insights so teams are value-maxing, not token-maxing',
  },
  {
    k: 'Credentials',
    v: 'Six AWS certifications, including DevOps Professional & Advanced Networking Specialty · B.S. Software Engineering, Iowa State (Cum Laude)',
  },
];

// Eyebrow styling reused for the two dated act labels. Inline so it wins over
// the global .content cascade without adding a new class or component.
const eyebrow =
  'font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--fg-dim); margin: 2.6em 0 0.3em;';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Cloud software engineer at John Deere making AI a first-class participant in the software development lifecycle. Earlier work spans API platforms, digital equipment licensing, cloud infrastructure, and developer experience."
>
  <article class="content mx-auto">
  <h1 class="type-h1 mb-6" style="color: var(--fg);">
    I’m Mitch. I build the <em style="color: var(--accent);">platforms around coding agents.</em>
  </h1>

  <dl class="fact-grid">
    {
      glance.map((row) => (
        <div>
          <dt class="skills-cat">{row.k}</dt>
          <dd class="skills-list">{row.v}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <div>
      <dt class="skills-cat">Links</dt>
      <dd class="fact-links skills-list">
        <a href={site.github} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" {...linkProps}>GitHub</a>
        <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);"> · </span>
        <a href={site.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" {...linkProps}>LinkedIn</a>
        <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);"> · </span>
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`} {...linkProps}>Email</a>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

    <p style={eyebrow}>Recent work · October 2025 – October 2026</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">The AI-Era SDLC</h2>

    <p>
      I focus entirely on advancing John Deere’s DevAI and Agentic Development Lifecycle (ADLC)
      capabilities so Frontier Teams can move faster. We dogfood every idea on our own product first.
      AI Insights started that way.
    </p>

    <p>
      AI Insights is our proving ground. It brings AI usage across the company into view, from the
      organization down to the individual engineer. By pairing SDLC metrics with AI usage, it gives
      each engineer a tailored report so they can reflect on how they work with AI and decide what to
      change.
      <a href="/resume/">Read my experience or download my résumé →</a>
    </p>

    <h3>Production delivery</h3>
    <p>
      For a team-owned <a href="/work/agentic-engineering/">engineering evidence product</a>,
      I wrote the requirements and much of the design record, delivered key live-data views and
      a personal work timeline, and designed and implemented shared caching. The product is in
      production with steady, growing use. The caching decision balances latency, data freshness,
      and availability; the case study walks through that tradeoff and its acceptance checks.
    </p>

    <h3>Cloud execution and trust</h3>
    <p>
      I ran <a href="/work/secure-agent-execution/">Codex and Claude Code in cloud sandboxes</a>
      and added repository-credential proxying so the credential stays outside the execution
      environment. I also built a <a href="/work/agent-trust/">skill-scanning trust layer</a>
      and packaged reusable review tools. A separate keyless Codex pilot has merged; live verification
      and containment hardening remain in progress.
    </p>

    <h3>Operating the delivery platform</h3>
    <p>
      In late 2025 and early 2026, I <a href="/work/pr-to-production/">hardened the
      separation-of-duties platform</a> with Datadog monitors, dashboards, and synthetics, added
      automatic incidents for pipeline failures, and consolidated a legacy processor. I also worked
      through security findings and reviewed organization-governance changes.
    </p>

    <h3>Making the work reusable</h3>
    <p>
      I designed the <a href="/work/team-brain/">shared team brain</a> and its retrieval,
      graph, memory, and quality tooling. I wrote the team’s harness thesis and Agent Delegation
      Stack, and co-facilitate a guild and office hours for around 38 practitioners. Sourced briefs,
      repository instructions, review skills, and feedback hooks make that work available beyond
      an individual session.
    </p>
    <p>
      My working model is <strong>Agent = Model + Harness</strong>: model capability becomes
      useful through context, controls, tests, and feedback. I explore it in the
      <a href="/writing/the-model-is-table-stakes/">Agent Harness field guide</a>.
      Production adoption and observational signals do not establish causal productivity gains.
    </p>

    <p style={eyebrow}>Earlier foundations · 2019 – 2025</p>
    <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">From APIs to Enterprise Platforms</h2>

    <h3>2019 – 2021 · John Deere Financial</h3>
    <p>
      I started at John Deere Financial during an early attempt to make APIs safely available to
      dealer software. The ambition resembled what a product such as Azure API Management provides:
      a common gateway for governing and exposing APIs instead of another one-off integration. I
      architected the platform’s <a href="/work/global-gateway/">first external-facing API</a>
      for dealer access to sales-lead data, with role-based authorization and audit logging designed
      in from the beginning. That early platform later matured into a gateway used for
      both internal and external APIs.
    </p>

    <h3>2021 – 2023 · Digital Equipment Capabilities</h3>
    <p>
      I then moved closer to the technology on John Deere machinery. On <a href="/work/license-management/">equipment licensing</a>,
      I worked behind the customer experience for purchasing digital capabilities on equipment. A secure
      data-ingestion prototype I built
      during a hackathon showed that license data could be made useful to internal teams without
      giving up isolation or access controls, and helped make the case for a dedicated enterprise
      Data Engineering team.
    </p>

    <h3>2023 – 2025 · Enterprise Cloud Foundations</h3>
    <p>
      The next chapter moved from individual products to cloud foundations serving engineering
      teams across John Deere. I led network
      consolidation, orchestrated a DNS migration, automated
      certificate renewal, and replaced manual account and VPC deletion with an event-driven
      workflow. The work reduced operational drag and made recurring infrastructure tasks easier
      to perform consistently.
    </p>

    <p>
      The throughline across those chapters is the same instinct that now shapes my AI work: make the
      hard, important thing also the easy, default thing. In those chapters, that meant secure access
      nobody had to reinvent, compliance that did not depend on an engineer remembering it, renewals
      without a human in the loop, and onboarding through reproducible environments.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      I’m not interested in pulling humans out of the loop. The target is complementarity: higher
      confidence, higher quality, lower cognitive load on the person doing the work.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      That was true when the output was a pipeline nobody had to think about. It’s just as true now
      that the output is an agent.
    </p>

    <h2>Public implementation</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/work/bounded-runner/">bounded execution lab</a> gives you a small system to run,
      break, and inspect: a synthetic credential gateway, a deterministic worker, and a separate
      validator. Its source, tests, patch, and recorded fixture outcomes are public. It is independent
      work, not a reproduction of an internal system.</p>

    <h2>Education &amp; certifications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>B.S. Software Engineering, Iowa State University (Cum Laude, 2020). Cyber security minor.</li>
      {certs.map((c) => <li>{c.name} <span style="color: var(--fg-dim); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82em;"> · {c.year}</span></li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Capabilities in practice</h2>
    <div class="capability-groups">
      <section>
        <h3>Agent-assisted delivery</h3>
        <p>Harness design, context engineering, MCP, knowledge curation, and outcome-based engineering metrics. Built with Claude Code, Codex, and Copilot.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Cloud foundations</h3>
        <p>AWS, Terraform, CloudFormation, networking, DNS, and event-driven infrastructure. Security work spans OAuth, secrets, certificates, and compliance automation.</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Developer platforms</h3>
        <p>GitHub Actions, shared CI/CD workflows, Renovate, repository governance, Backstage, and Dev Containers. Observability through CloudWatch, Datadog, Splunk, Grafana, and X-Ray; implementation in Python, Java, JavaScript, and TypeScript.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      Built with Astro, Tailwind, and MDX; hosted on GitHub Pages. The source is public; see the
      link in the footer.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
